# 03-code-folder-structure-rules.mdc - Project Folder Structure Rules

## Common Policy

- MUST choose a folder structure optimized for the project type.
- MUST separate responsibilities by feature or layer.
- MUST separate environment-specific configuration through `dev/`, `prod/`, or centralized `config/`.
- MUST keep at least `README.md` and `.gitignore` at the project root unless the platform requires otherwise.
- MUST keep `tests/`, `docs/`, and `scripts/` as independent folders when the project has tests, documentation, or scripts.
- MUST limit nesting to 2 responsibility levels by default, such as `src/features/` or `app/services/`.
- MAY exceed 2 responsibility levels only when the framework requires it, such as Java package paths or generated project files.
- SHOULD copy and adapt the matching language template when introducing a new project structure.

## JavaScript and TypeScript

- SHOULD use this structure for Node.js or React projects when applicable:

```text
project/
├── public/
├── src/
│   ├── components/
│   ├── pages/
│   ├── hooks/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── utils/
│   └── types/
├── tests/
├── scripts/
├── .env
└── README.md
```

- SHOULD use `public/` for static assets when the project serves web assets.
- SHOULD keep application source under `src/`.
- SHOULD use `components/`, `pages/`, `hooks/`, `services/`, `utils/`, and `types/` inside `src/` when those responsibilities exist.
- SHOULD keep tests under `tests/` and build or CI helpers under `scripts/`.

## Python

- SHOULD use this structure for Web, API, or data-processing projects when applicable:

```text
project/
├── app/
│   ├── api/
│   ├── models/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── core/
│   └── utils/
├── tests/
├── scripts/
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md
```

- SHOULD use `app/` for Web, API, or data-processing application code.
- SHOULD use `app/api`, `app/models`, `app/services`, `app/core`, and `app/utils` when those responsibilities exist.
- SHOULD keep pytest or equivalent tests under `tests/`.
- SHOULD keep batch jobs and utility scripts under `scripts/`.

## C Sharp

- SHOULD use the Unity structure with `Assets/`, `Assets/Scripts`, `Assets/Art`, `Assets/Prefabs`, `ProjectSettings/`, and `README.md`.
- MUST follow Unity-required folders such as `Assets/` and `ProjectSettings/` for Unity projects.
- SHOULD place Unity scripts, art or audio assets, and prefabs under `Assets/Scripts`, `Assets/Art`, and `Assets/Prefabs` when applicable.
- SHOULD use this non-Unity .NET structure when applicable:

```text
project/
├── src/
│   └── AppName/
│       ├── Controllers/
│       ├── Models/
│       ├── Services/
│       └── Program.cs
├── tests/
├── scripts/
└── README.md
```

- SHOULD use `src/<AppName>/Controllers`, `Models`, `Services`, and `Program.cs` for non-Unity .NET projects when applicable.

## Other Language Layouts

- SHOULD use `src/main/java`, `src/main/resources`, `src/test`, and `build.gradle` or `pom.xml` for Java projects when applicable.
- SHOULD use `cmd/`, `internal/`, `pkg/`, `api/`, `scripts/`, and `go.mod` for Go projects when applicable.
- SHOULD use `src/main.rs`, `src/lib.rs`, `src/modules`, `tests/`, and `Cargo.toml` for Rust projects when applicable.
- SHOULD use `Project.xcodeproj`, `Sources/`, `Resources/`, and `Tests/` for Swift iOS projects when applicable.

## Shared and Operations

- MAY use `shared/` for common resources across languages.
- SHOULD centralize CI/CD settings and environment files under `config/`.
- SHOULD copy and adjust these templates instead of inventing an unrelated structure for new projects.
